<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cerro Rico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1545</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Cerro Rico of Potosí, Sumaq Urqu, Cerro de Potosí</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cerro Rico ('rich hill') is a mountain above the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits were discovered by the Spanish in 1545. Worked by indigenous labourers under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a> system, it became the largest single source of silver in the world in the late sixteenth century and supplied much of the bullion that flowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Mining there continues today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cerro-rico.jpg" alt="Cerro Rico" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mhwater at Dutch Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerro_ricco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
